# Admin Dashboard — Recent Activity & Quizzes Page Handoff Notes

**Scope:** Wire up the Dashboard tab's "Recent Activity" placeholder, fix a
crash on the Quizzes tab, add search/filter tooling to the Quizzes tab,
reorganize + relabel that page for a non-technical admin, apply the same
fixes and visual pass to the Subjects tab, then add cascade delete (with
a hard-to-click-through-by-accident confirmation) so deleting a subject
or quiz also cleans up everything under it.

**Files touched:** `admin.js` (aka `dashboard.js` — updated), `admin.css`
(updated), `quizzes.js` (updated), `quizzes.css` (updated), `subjects.js`
(updated), `subjects.css` (updated), `questions.js` (reviewed only, no
changes needed), `cascadeDelete.js` (new), `dangerDialog.js` (new).

---

## 1. Dashboard tab — "Recent Activity" made functional

Previously a static placeholder (`🚀 Quiz Arena Admin is ready.` /
`Create subjects, quizzes and questions to get started.`). Now pulls real
data.

- **Source:** most recent 5 docs each from `subjects`, `quizzes`, and
  `questions`, ordered by `createdAt` (all three collections confirmed to
  have this field). No dedicated activity-log collection — this is a
  fan-out read across the three content collections, merged and sorted
  client-side, top 6 shown. Cheap at current scale; if it ever needs to
  scale up, a written activity-log collection would be the next step
  instead of widening the per-type fetch limit.
- **Display:** icon + `"New <type> added/created: <title>"` + relative
  timestamp (`"5m ago"` → `"2h ago"` → `"3d ago"` → falls back to a plain
  date past a week).
- **Field lookups are defensive**, not hard-coded to one field name: tries
  `title` then `name` for subjects, `title` for quizzes, `text` then
  `question` for questions, so a naming difference doesn't silently render
  `undefined`.
- **Race-condition guard:** if the admin switches tabs while the fetch is
  in flight, bails instead of writing into DOM nodes that no longer exist
  (same pattern as the existing `loadDashboardStats()` guard).
- Wrapped in try/catch — a failed query shows "Couldn't load recent
  activity right now." instead of breaking the dashboard.
- Output is escaped (`escapeHtml`) before being injected via `innerHTML`,
  since subject/quiz/question titles are admin-authored but still get
  rendered as raw text.

**Fetch limit vs. display limit:** fetches 5 most recent per collection
(15 total), displays top 6 after merging/sorting. Worth noting: if a
single type dominates recent activity (e.g. 6 quizzes created back-to-back
with nothing else happening), the 5-per-type fetch limit could theoretically
undercount that one type in the top-6 display. Not fixed yet — flagged as
outstanding below.

## 2. Quizzes tab — crash fixed: tab-switch race condition

**Symptom:**
```
quizzes.js:79 Uncaught (in promise) TypeError: Cannot read properties of
null (reading 'addEventListener')
```

**Root cause:** `renderQuizzes(container)` awaits `loadSubjects()` then
`loadQuizzes()` before wiring up form/button listeners. If the admin
switches tabs while either await is in flight, `renderAdminDashboard()`
replaces the entire `app.innerHTML` — including a brand-new
`#adminContent` — so the **old** `container` node is now detached from the
live document. When the pending call resumed and did
`document.getElementById("cancelEditBtn")`, that's a *global* document
lookup that no longer finds the (detached) element, so it returned `null`.

Same class of bug as the tab-switch guards already in `admin.js`'s
`loadDashboardStats()`/`loadRecentActivity()` — just not yet applied to
`quizzes.js`.

**Fix:** every DOM read/write in `quizzes.js` now goes through a
module-level `currentContainer` reference (captured at the top of
`renderQuizzes()`) via `container.querySelector(...)`, instead of the
global `document.getElementById(...)`. Writing into a detached subtree is
harmless (invisible, wasted work) instead of a crash. Added explicit
`currentContainer !== container` / `container.isConnected` bail-outs after
every `await` that's followed by a DOM write (form submit, delete, load).

**Note for later:** `subjects.js` and `questions.js` were *not* reviewed —
they likely have the identical bug shape (await inside a render function,
then `document.getElementById`). Flagged, not yet fixed.

## 3. Quizzes tab — search/filter toolbar added

Per request, gave the admin Quizzes page the same kind of toolbar
Marketplace has: search box, subject filter pills, Level/Semester
dropdowns.

- **First pass** reused Marketplace's actual CSS classes
  (`marketplace-header`, `marketplace-toolbar`, etc.) directly in
  `quizzes.js`'s markup, on the assumption `marketplace.css` is loaded
  globally in the SPA (same as `admin.css`/`dashboard.css` were shown to
  be, per the original handoff notes' §3 collision writeup).
- **Reworked per request** — Quizzes now has **its own classes**
  (`quiz-toolbar`, `quiz-search`, `quiz-filters`, `quiz-filter-btn`,
  `quiz-level-semester-filters`, `quiz-level-filter`,
  `quiz-semester-filter`) with matching rules added to `quizzes.css`
  itself. This page no longer depends on `marketplace.css` being loaded
  at all — avoids the kind of cross-file organization problem the
  original handoff notes were explicitly trying to prevent with the
  `.admin-wrap` scoping pattern.
- **Filtering is entirely client-side** against the already-fetched
  `quizzesCache` (no extra Firestore reads on filter change) — same
  pattern as `marketplace.js`.
- Picking a Level or Semester resets the subject filter and narrows which
  subject pills are shown, mirroring Marketplace's UX.
- Admin's dropdowns default to **"All Levels" / "All Semesters"**
  (Marketplace defaults to a specific level/semester since it's scoped to
  one student; the admin view needs to see everything by default). This
  was a judgment call, not confirmed with the client — flagged below.
- Empty state now distinguishes "no quizzes exist yet" from "no quizzes
  match your filters."

### Bug: "undefined" showing in Level/Semester dropdowns

**Cause:** some quiz docs predate the `level`/`semester` fields. Without a
fallback, `String(undefined)` becomes the literal text `"undefined"`,
which is exactly what rendered as a dropdown option.

**Fix:** applied the same backward-compatibility default
`marketplace.js`'s `loadActiveQuizzes()` already uses for this exact
situation — `level ?? 100`, `semester ?? 2` — when building
`quizzesCache`. Consistent with existing convention rather than a new
guess, but worth confirming this default (100 Level / Semester 2) is
right for the admin view specifically, vs. e.g. showing those as an
explicit "Unspecified" bucket instead of silently folding them into 100/2.

## 4. Quizzes tab — reordered and relabeled for a non-technical admin

Previous order: header+toolbar → Create Quiz form → quiz list. Per
request, reordered and gave each section its own heading so someone who
isn't a web developer can tell at a glance what each part does:

1. **"Add a New Quiz"** (moved to the top) — the create/edit form, with a
   plain-English subtitle: *"Fill in the details below, then press
   'Create Quiz' to publish it."*
2. A visible divider (`border-top`) separates the two sections.
3. **"All Quizzes"** (moved below the form) — search box, filter pills,
   Level/Semester dropdowns, and the quiz list, with its own subtitle:
   *"Search, filter, edit or delete the quizzes you've already created."*

No functional/JS logic changed here — purely markup order, headings, and
the CSS divider.

## 5. Bug: form inputs overflowing their box in "Add a New Quiz"

**Symptom (reported):** input/select boxes in the create-quiz form
stretched out past the edge of their card.

**Root cause:** `.quiz-form-grid` is a 4-column CSS Grid
(`repeat(4, 1fr)`). Grid items default to `min-width: auto`, which
resolves to the item's *intrinsic content size* — for the Subject
`<select>`, that's driven by its longest `<option>` text (e.g. `"Physics
• 100 Level • First Semester"`). Once that intrinsic width exceeds the
column's `1fr` share, the column — and the input inside it — gets pushed
wider than the grid track, overflowing the form. Same root cause as the
header-truncation fix in the original handoff notes (§9), just the CSS
Grid version of the flexbox `min-width: auto` gotcha instead of the flex
one.

**Fix:**
- `.form-field { min-width: 0; }` — lets the grid column shrink below its
  content's intrinsic width instead of forcing the track wider.
- `.quiz-form select, .quiz-form input { width: 100%; box-sizing:
  border-box; }` — makes sure padding/border are included in the declared
  width instead of added on top of it, and that they actually fill their
  (now-correctly-sized) column.

## 6. Subjects tab — two bugs fixed

Reviewed per the §2 note above (flagged as likely sharing the Quizzes
crash). Found that plus one worse bug.

### Bug: delete was completely broken

**Cause:** the delete button's markup never set `data-id="${docSnap.id}"`.
`deleteSubject(e)` read `e.target.dataset.id`, which was always
`undefined`, so every delete click called
`deleteDoc(doc(db, "subjects", undefined))` — a malformed path that
throws rather than deleting anything. This wasn't a race condition or
edge case; delete was non-functional for every subject, every time.

**Fix:** added `data-id="${docSnap.id}"` to the button, and switched the
handler to read `e.currentTarget.dataset.id` (safer than `e.target` if
markup inside the button ever changes) with a defensive check that logs
clearly instead of letting Firestore throw a confusing error if the id is
ever missing again.

### Bug: same tab-switch race as Quizzes (§2)

`loadSubjects()` captured `#subjectsList` via `document.getElementById`
*before* its `await getDocs(...)`, then did a fresh global
`document.getElementById("subjectCount")` lookup *after* the await —
exactly the pattern that crashed `quizzes.js`. If the admin switched tabs
while that fetch was in flight, `#subjectCount` would be null post-await
and `.textContent = ...` would throw.

**Fix:** same `currentContainer`-scoped pattern as `quizzes.js` — all DOM
access goes through `container.querySelector(...)`, with
`currentContainer !== container` / `container.isConnected` guards after
every `await`.

### Smaller fix: `renderSubjects()` wasn't actually async

It was declared `async` but never `await`ed `loadSubjects()`, so a caller
awaiting `renderSubjects(container)` would resolve before data actually
loaded. Now properly `await`s it.

**`questions.js` still not reviewed** — carried forward in Outstanding.

## 7. Subjects tab — visual pass to match Dashboard/Quizzes

Per request, brought Subjects' look in line with the quieter,
labeled, institutional style already applied to Dashboard and Quizzes.

- **Split into two labeled sections**, same pattern as Quizzes: "Add a
  New Subject" (form) on top, a `border-top` divider, "All Subjects"
  (count badge + list) below.
- **Form fields now have labels** above each input/select in a proper
  grid (`subject-form-grid` / `subject-form-field`), replacing the
  original bare unlabeled row of controls.
- **Card hover toned down** — was `translateY(-3px)` with a heavy
  `0 10px 30px` shadow; now matches `quiz-card`'s subtler
  `translateY(-2px)` + lighter shadow.
- **Delete button restyled** from a solid red button to the same quiet
  light-red-background/dark-red-text treatment `quizzes.css` uses for its
  own delete button.
- Dropped the "📚" emoji from the page heading to match the plain
  "Add a New Quiz" / "All Quizzes" heading style (no emoji) used on the
  Quizzes page.
- Removed dead CSS: the original file had a `.subject-meta-grid` /
  `.subject-meta-field` block that was never referenced by the markup —
  replaced by the actually-used `subject-form-grid` / `subject-form-field`.
- **New classes are Subjects-specific** (`subject-form-grid`,
  `subject-form-field`, `subject-form-field-wide`, `subject-add-btn`) —
  deliberately *not* reusing `quizzes.css`'s bare `.form-field` /
  `.form-field-wide`, since both stylesheets load together in the SPA and
  identical bare names would recreate the exact cross-page collision
  problem documented in the original handoff notes (§3/§8).

**Note for later:** `quizzes.css` itself still defines `.form-field` /
`.form-field-wide` as *bare, unprefixed* classes — a latent collision
risk for any future page that reuses those names, same shape as the bug
already fixed once elsewhere. Flagged, not fixed — out of scope for this
round.

## 8. `questions.js` reviewed — already fixed, no changes needed

Followed up on the §2/§6 flag. Turns out `questions.js` had already been
brought up to the same `currentContainer`-scoped, tab-switch-race-guarded
pattern used in `quizzes.js` and `subjects.js` — every DOM read/write
already goes through `container.querySelector(...)` with guards after
every `await`. Nothing to fix here.

Confirmed while reviewing: the `questions` collection links back to its
quiz via a **`quizId`** field (`where("quizId", "==", quizId)`), which is
what made §9 below possible without guessing at the schema.

## 9. Cascade delete — deleting a subject/quiz now cleans up what's under it

Per request: previously, deleting a subject left its quizzes behind
(pointing at a subject that no longer exists), and deleting a quiz left
its questions behind — both silently orphaned in Firestore, forever.
Now both cascade properly, with a deliberately-hard-to-click-through
confirmation first.

- **Delete a subject** → deletes every quiz under it (which in turn
  deletes every question under *those* quizzes) → then the subject
  itself. Three levels: Subject → Quizzes → Questions.
- **Delete a quiz** → deletes every question under it → then the quiz
  itself. Two levels: Quiz → Questions. Used both directly (Quizzes tab)
  and as the building block for each quiz when a subject cascades.
- **Deleting a single question** is unchanged — a question has no
  children, so it still just uses a plain `confirm()`.

### New shared modules

- **`cascadeDelete.js`** — the count/delete logic. `countSubjectCascade()`
  / `countQuizCascade()` are read-only, used to put real numbers in the
  warning dialog. `deleteSubjectCascade()` / `deleteQuizCascade()` do the
  actual deletes. Counts are re-fetched fresh right before deleting
  (not reused from the earlier count call), so a quiz/question added in
  the few seconds the dialog was open can't cause a mismatch between what
  was shown and what gets deleted. Deletes are plain `deleteDoc()` calls,
  not batched writes — fine at this app's scale; if a subject/quiz ever
  has hundreds of descendants, Firestore's 500-operation batch limit
  would matter and this would need `writeBatch()`.

- **`dangerDialog.js`** — the confirmation UI, shared by `subjects.js`
  and `quizzes.js`. Per request, this isn't a plain `confirm()`: it shows
  the exact counts of what will also be deleted, says "This cannot be
  undone," and **requires typing the exact subject/quiz name** before the
  "Delete Permanently" button enables — deliberately more friction than a
  single click, so it's hard to get through on autopilot. Renders as an
  overlay appended directly to `document.body` (not into any tab's own
  container), specifically so it's unaffected by the tab-switch DOM
  replacement race documented in §2 — it's an independent element until
  it explicitly closes itself.

### Bug caught while wiring this up: missing `.btn-secondary` style

`dangerDialog.js`'s Cancel button uses `.btn-secondary`, but that class
is normally only defined in `quizzes.css`. Since the dialog is shared by
both `subjects.js` and `quizzes.js` and renders outside any one tab's
markup, it shouldn't depend on `quizzes.css` specifically being loaded
for its Cancel button to look right — the same cross-file dependency
problem flagged earlier with the Marketplace classes (§3/§7). Fixed by
duplicating a `.btn-secondary` definition directly into `admin.css`
(where the rest of the dialog's styles and `.btn-danger` already live),
matching this project's existing convention of each stylesheet owning
its own copy of shared button styles rather than depending on another
file's.

### Known limitation: Cloudinary images aren't cleaned up

`deleteQuizCascade()` deletes question *documents* but not the
Cloudinary images those questions reference (`question.image` URLs).
Deleting from Cloudinary needs a signed admin-API call, which this
client-side code has no credentials for — only the unsigned upload
preset used to *create* images. Orphaned images will remain in
Cloudinary storage after a cascade delete runs. Flagged, not solved.

---

